# Rank 1764 / CVE-2023-51662 (GHSA-hwcc-4cv8-cf3h, CWE-295):
# snowflakedb/snowflake-connector-net wires its connection-string option into
# the HTTP client's certificate-revocation check with the polarity inverted.
# SFSessionHttpClientProperties.BuildHttpClientConfig
# (Snowflake.Data/Core/Session/SFSessionHttpClientProperties.cs:75) passes the
# permissive-polarity field straight through:
#
#   internal HttpClientConfig BuildHttpClientConfig()
#   {
#       return new HttpClientConfig(
#           insecureMode,          // <- param 1 is `crlCheckEnabled`
#           proxyProperties.proxyHost,
#           ...
#
# HttpClientConfig's first parameter is `crlCheckEnabled`
# (Snowflake.Data/Core/HttpUtil.cs:22), consumed one hop later as
#
#   httpHandler = new HttpClientHandler()
#   {
#       CheckCertificateRevocationList = config.CrlCheckEnabled,
#
# The option and the setting assert opposite polarities: `insecureMode == true`
# means "relax verification" while `crlCheckEnabled == true` means "perform the
# check". Joining them without negation inverts the setting both ways -- the
# default connection (`insecureMode` unset, false) ran with
# CheckCertificateRevocationList = false, so every connection skipped the CRL
# check and a MITM holding a revoked certificate for the account's host is
# accepted, reading credentials off the wire; explicitly requesting insecure
# mode is the only spelling that turns the check on. Nothing in the
# application is misused and no untrusted data flows anywhere; the application
# asked for the secure default and got the insecure one.
#
# 49cb77ddd6e18c110eca35aa580e89d73c46cc33 (SNOW-961434, PR #832) changes the
# one argument to `!insecureMode` and extends the property tests to assert the
# polarity in both directions. The remediation is present at the commit, the
# bare `insecureMode` at its parent, so both halves of the 1390 check hold; the
# pool row is correct in every field and needs no correction.
#
# What fires today. `timeout 180 ./bin/vyql scan --cache off --max-ram 6GB` over
# both real checkouts reports byte-identical output: 117 findings each, the same
# per-rule split (VYQL-CFG-014 x60, VYQL-DOS-001 x10, VYQL-SEC-002 x9,
# VYQL-NUM-005 x9, VYQL-PATH-001 x8, VYQL-RES-001 x7, singletons below), stable
# over three runs per side, and `scan -flags only` likewise identical at 152
# flags each. SFSessionHttpClientProperties.cs carries no label beyond the
# generic library param-source; the only rows in HttpUtil.cs are a
# WeakRandomValue at :523 and an UncheckedEncodedLengthArithmetic at :341, the
# retry backoff and the URL encoder -- findings in the file are not coverage.
#
# This is the C# instance of the weakness class rank 1405 filed for Rust
# (CVE-2025-11695, mongo-rust-driver `tlsInsecure` negated into
# `allow_invalid_certificates`): a security setting assigned the logical
# negation -- or here the non-negation -- of the option that requests it.
# code.CertValidationDisabled (VYQL-CFG-004, CWE-295) is the right existing home
# and nothing else in the CWE-295 set fits: core.CertificateValidation is the
# neutralising control, and the ProxyRedirectCertVerificationBypass /
# CertValidationDependency / SnsCertificateUrlTrustBypass /
# JavaMailSmtpSslIdentityVerificationMissing shapes are other protocols or
# redirect hops. No concept or rule is minted here, because the measurements
# below say nothing that would discriminate can be built on one.
#
# ATTENTION, and the blocker is a frontend fact gap plus a destination that is
# not an API. Both are measured on this rank, not inherited from 1405:
#
# (1) The C# walk emits no token for a negation. csStructuredContextTokens
#     (internal/extract/frontend/treesitter/csharp.go:455) has cases for
#     assignments, declarations, invocations, object creations, member accesses,
#     identifiers, element accesses, binary/cast/checked expressions and
#     literals -- no prefix_unary_expression -- so `!insecureMode` contributes
#     exactly the tokens `insecureMode` alone would, and csExprShape (:599)
#     returns "" for the unary kind as well. Measured with probe bindings on a
#     minimal repro: `node.context.identifier contains "insecureMode"` and
#     `callee.path == "HttpClientConfig"` both match BOTH revisions; the
#     function-context token streams are the same list.
# (2) No argument-position predicate reaches an object-creation call's
#     arguments. `args.any.literal contains "proxy"` fires on an
#     invocation_expression carrying that literal and not on
#     `new HttpClientConfig(..., "proxy.example.com", ...)` -- the arg routes
#     are live for invocations and inert for object creation -- and there is no
#     args[N] shape or kind predicate at all, so "argument 0 is a bare field
#     read, not a unary" is not a statement the binding language can make.
# (3) The graph keeps the distinction and nothing can read it: the vulnerable
#     form lowers to `Elem --FLOWS--> Arg --FLOWS--> ...#param#crlCheckEnabled`
#     and the fixed one to `Unary path=__unary.not` feeding the same parameter
#     (vyql graph over the pair, case (d)). The discriminating fact exists at
#     the node level and dies between the USG and the binding layer.
# (4) Even with (1)-(3) fixed, the destination's polarity is only knowable from
#     a private class's own parameter name: HttpClientConfig is Snowflake's
#     `internal class`, undocumented, so a binding keyed on it answers to this
#     incident rather than to any API documentation -- the genericity test
#     fails outright. The documented .NET surface one hop down,
#     HttpClientHandler.CheckCertificateRevocationList, is assigned
#     `config.CrlCheckEnabled` at BOTH revisions (HttpUtil.cs:143, untouched by
#     the fixed revision), so a sink binding there scores 1 -> 1; and .NET's documented
#     default for the property is false, so "not forced to literal true" is the
#     platform norm rather than a defect signal -- flagging it would fire on
#     every configurable client and still not separate these revisions.
# (5) The 1380 counterweight is negative: the patched method's only content is
#     the constructor call itself, no API-documented control remains inside it,
#     and no check moved anywhere -- the change is one token in the same function,
#     so this is not the delegated-check family either.
#
# Not judged mechanical. A prefix_unary_expression case in the walk and shape
# function would be small, but it is a new fact class shared by every C# scan,
# and it does not by itself unlock the rank: the polarity of the destination
# parameter is not data in any layer, and correlating "unnegated flag" with
# "strict-polarity parameter" would be a rule over private identifier
# spellings, the shape AGENTS.md assigns to neither layer. The Rust sibling of
# this gap (1405) is judged not mechanical for the same reach reason.
#
# The four cases: (a) and (b) are the two real revisions and record the double
# miss. (c) is the correct conversion at the documented API -- the strict
# setting derived by negating the permissive flag -- and must stay unflagged.
# (d) is the frontend pair for (3): the same constructor fed the bare field and
# the negated field, and a plain method call carrying a literal, showing the arg
# route live for invocations and absent for object creation.
#
# verify_current_expected: attention-engine-gap, all four cases rejected. (c)
# and (d) are correct code and must remain rejects; (a) flips to expect once a
# negation fact and an argument-position predicate exist together.

test "rank1764: (a) a permissive-polarity connection option is passed un-negated into the strict-polarity certificate-check parameter, so the default connection skips the CRL check"
  lang csharp
  file Snowflake.Data/Core/Session/SFSessionHttpClientProperties.cs
  reject VYQL-CFG-004
  reject VYQL-CFG-023
  code
  ```
  namespace Snowflake.Data.Core
  {
      internal class HttpClientConfig
      {
          public HttpClientConfig(
              bool crlCheckEnabled,
              string proxyHost,
              string proxyPort,
              string proxyUser,
              string proxyPassword,
              string noProxyList,
              bool disableRetry,
              bool forceRetryOn404,
              int maxHttpRetries,
              bool includeRetryReason = true)
          {
              CrlCheckEnabled = crlCheckEnabled;
          }

          public readonly bool CrlCheckEnabled;
      }

      internal class SFSessionHttpClientProperties
      {
          internal bool insecureMode;
          internal string proxyHost;
          internal string proxyPort;

          internal HttpClientConfig BuildHttpClientConfig()
          {
              return new HttpClientConfig(
                  insecureMode,
                  proxyHost,
                  proxyPort,
                  null,
                  null,
                  null,
                  false,
                  false,
                  7,
                  true);
          }
      }
  }
  ```

test "rank1764: (b) the fixed revision negates the option on the way into the strict-polarity parameter"
  lang csharp
  file Snowflake.Data/Core/Session/SFSessionHttpClientProperties.cs
  reject VYQL-CFG-004
  reject VYQL-CFG-023
  code
  ```
  namespace Snowflake.Data.Core
  {
      internal class HttpClientConfig
      {
          public HttpClientConfig(
              bool crlCheckEnabled,
              string proxyHost,
              string proxyPort,
              string proxyUser,
              string proxyPassword,
              string noProxyList,
              bool disableRetry,
              bool forceRetryOn404,
              int maxHttpRetries,
              bool includeRetryReason = true)
          {
              CrlCheckEnabled = crlCheckEnabled;
          }

          public readonly bool CrlCheckEnabled;
      }

      internal class SFSessionHttpClientProperties
      {
          internal bool insecureMode;
          internal string proxyHost;
          internal string proxyPort;

          internal HttpClientConfig BuildHttpClientConfig()
          {
              return new HttpClientConfig(
                  !insecureMode,
                  proxyHost,
                  proxyPort,
                  null,
                  null,
                  null,
                  false,
                  false,
                  7,
                  true);
          }
      }
  }
  ```

test "rank1764: (c) a strict-polarity documented setting derived by negating a permissive option is the correct conversion and must not be flagged"
  lang csharp
  file Snowflake.Data/Core/HttpUtil.cs
  reject VYQL-CFG-004
  reject VYQL-CFG-023
  code
  ```
  using System.Net.Http;

  namespace Snowflake.Data.Core
  {
      internal class HandlerFactory
      {
          internal bool insecureMode;

          internal HttpMessageHandler SetupCustomHttpHandler()
          {
              return new HttpClientHandler()
              {
                  // The strict-polarity setting, negated from the permissive option
                  CheckCertificateRevocationList = !insecureMode,
                  UseCookies = false,
                  UseProxy = false
              };
          }
      }
  }
  ```

test "rank1764: (d) the frontend pair: the same constructor fed the bare field and the negated field, plus a plain invocation whose argument route is live -- the negation and the argument position are both invisible on the object-creation call"
  lang csharp
  file Snowflake.Data/Core/Pair.cs
  reject VYQL-CFG-004
  reject VYQL-CFG-023
  code
  ```
  using System;

  namespace Snowflake.Data.Core
  {
      internal class Pair
      {
          internal bool insecureMode;

          internal HttpClientConfig BareField()
          {
              return new HttpClientConfig(insecureMode, null, null, null, null, null, false, false, 7, true);
          }

          internal HttpClientConfig NegatedField()
          {
              return new HttpClientConfig(!insecureMode, null, null, null, null, null, false, false, 7, true);
          }

          internal void InvocationWithLiteral()
          {
              Console.WriteLine("proxy.example.com");
          }
      }
  }
  ```
